---
title: Chainmail and Charizards
---

<script lang="ts">
  import { InfoTable, Heading } from "$lib/ui/elements"
</script>

This supplement is an adaptation of the original [Chainmail and Charizards](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hbqYJeEtULByAs5sPYT5u88T_7Yuf8GsuPs4ZVfmjZU/edit?tab=t.0) homebrew. This is a set of modifications to the Pokémon 5e rules for playing with pokémon in the classic Dungeons and Dragons setting, and integrating more with the D&D classes.

<Heading level="2" id="introduction">Introduction</Heading>

<Heading level="3" id="philosophy">Philosophy and Goals of This Document</Heading>

_Trainers as a Class_: Trainers are intended to be one class among the bunch. Becoming a trainer requires class levels. Not everybody is a trainer, nor are you required to be a trainer to be powerful. Instead, being a Trainer is one path to power alongside the study of blade or magic.

_Minimal Interference with Standard D&D_: I want the system to be unchanged when Pokemon are not around and play just like regular 5e. You do not need to rework the whole 5e system to include Pokemon in it, nor does it necessarily need to be the focus of any campaign. You should be able to play a Trainer in a normal module with little to no change.

_Cool vs. Awesome_: The ultimate motivation of this document is to see Wizards fight Pokemon Trainers and awesome stuff like that, have a bunch of different classes with a diverse set of powers coexist, compete, and grow parallel to each other.

_Introducing Rules, Moves, and Feats for Pokemon interacting with Standard D&D stuff_: Self-explanatory. I want to, if possible, add features to the game for how Pokemon interact with standard fantasy and the like. This is both to add new possibilities that wouldn’t be available in either system and also to clarify what happens when the two meet.


<Heading level="3" id="analysis-of-the-class">Analysis of the Class</Heading>

I’ve done my best to modify the Trainer class in Poke5e to make it balanced for regular 5e. Here are some points to note about its mechanics and the philosophy for how the class should work:

_Pokemon vs Adventurers_: For this class to be balanced, it’s imperative that Pokemon are weaker than individual PCs who took an equal amount of levels in a more traditional class. After all, a Trainer has a team, but a Fighter or a Wizard only have their skills with blade or spell. In addition, a Trainer should need their team, not just a single Pokemon. As such, a Pokemon should be akin to a specialized weapon or spell rather than a full-blown PC in its own right.

Fortunately, it doesn’t take many changes for this to work out. In general, a Pokemon is about as powerful as a PC one “tier” below them as defined in D&D. For example, a “Tier 2” Pokemon (levels 5-10) has strength comparable to a “Tier 1” PC (levels 1-4). This means that a Pokemon is still powerful, but will not overshadow the rest of the party. Their hit points generally do keep up with PCs, though their offensive capabilities do lag behind quite a bit.

_Moves vs Spells and Weapons_: Pokemon moves, even the really basic ones like ‘tackle’, are almost always better than cantrips (Eldritch Blast excluded). Weaker Pokemon moves used by lower-level Pokemon tend to be on par with simple weapon attacks.

Spells generally outshine Pokemon moves in terms of damage output, but Pokemon moves can get rather good AOE effects even if they aren’t doing a lot of damage (acid is 30’ cone, for example, and some Pokemon learn it quite early). The strongest Pokemon moves, such as a 3 PP move being used by a level 20 Pokemon, tend to be pretty comparable with 5th level spells.

_The Swiss Army Knife_: What’s a Trainer’s role, then? Honestly, a Trainer can do pretty much anything with the right build or team. For just about any role, there’s a Pokemon that can do it. A beefier Pokemon like a Ryhorn can serve as an improvised Fighter. A Fighting-Type can serve as a Monk. A Fire or Electric Pokemon can easily serve as an Evocation Wizard. Naturally, a Pokemon will never be quite as good as a PC with traditional class levels at what the class specializes in. But the point is, they can still fill the role quite adequately.

Some roles are easier than others, though. For example, there are relatively few healing moves. Though, if you can set it up right and. There is also the Nurse subclass of Pokemon Trainer, which actually makes for a very good healer, too.

The easiest role is a tank, by contrast, as most Pokemon evolve into bulkier and tankier forms. As said above, their hit points do keep up with PCs rather well at least once they start evolving.

Mechanics aside, the Pokemon Trainer can also fit a diverse flavor. For example, a Trainer specializing in Grass Types can fit alongside Druids and Rangers. A Ghost-type Trainer can fit in with evil cultists and necromancers, and a Fighting-Type Trainer would be right at home with Monks. A future character I’m planning is a Dragon-Type Trainer who’s a priest who uses their Pokemon to kill and eat devils.

In my own setting I tend to have Trainers be considered a hybrid between a “Primal” class (such as a Druid, Ranger, Barbarian, and especially the Primeval Champion) and an Arcanist. So you tend to find them hanging around Magic Schools and Tribes.

_A Quadratic Class_: Pokemon Trainers at low levels are somewhat weak. Pokemon at low levels are fragile with a d6 hit die, and the most basic moves are usually only as strong as simple weapons. However, once you start leveling up, your power will start growing pretty rapidly. Especially once you level up. It’s around level 4 that the class starts getting particularly good. In addition, as you level up your Pokemon starts learning moves that do more diverse things like AOE effects and conditions.

_Trainer Vulnerability_: The best way to take out a Trainer is to go straight for the trainer themselves. As such, it’s actually fairly unlikely a Trainer will ever run out of Pokemon. Once they lose two or so Pokemon, and as other allies fall around them, the enemy will likely close in on the Trainer and that’ll be the end of it. As such, someone fighting a Trainer should take advantage of this, and a Trainer should try to stay behind their tankier members like their Fighter or Paladin. If you can force the enemy to fight all of your Pokemon, then you’re very likely to win.


<Heading level="2" id="pokemon-and-trainer-modifications">Pokémon and Trainer Modifications</Heading>

<Heading level="3" id="pokemon-hp">Pokémon HP</Heading>

Pokemon hit points are calculated differently. Calculate a Pokemon’s HP like you would a PC with the same hit die. They do not get any bonus hit points.

<Heading level="3" id="trainer-xp">Trainer XP</Heading>

Don’t bother dividing XP up for all your Pokemon and the like. Instead, just consider the Trainer and all their Pokemon to share a single XP score. A Trainer’s Pokemon have a level equal to their master’s Trainer Level.

<Heading level="3" id="commanding-pokemon">Commanding Pokémon</Heading>

Pokemon and their Trainers no longer share an action.

<Heading level="3" id="new-proficiencies">New Proficiencies</Heading>

Pokemon Trainers are proficient in light armor and simple weapons.

<Heading level="3" id="equipment">Equipment</Heading>

Pokemon Trainers do not start with all those Pokebucks. It’s quite a lot of money.

They also get a suit of leather armor and a simple weapon of their choice.

<Heading level="3" id="currency-conversion">Currency Conversion</Heading>

Speaking of which, a good conversion of gold to pokebucks is that 1 gold piece = 4 pokebucks. This calculation is determined by the fact that a potion of lesser healing in D&D 5e costs 50 gold pieces, whereas a potion in Poke5e that does the same thing costs 200 pokebucks.

<Heading level="3" id="trainerless-pokemon">Trainerless Pokemon</Heading>

If a Pokemon is separated from its trainer and cannot hear commands from them, then they act somewhat erratically. Assign a number between 1-4 (or 1-5 if they have extra move) to each of their moves. At the start of their turn, roll a d4 or a d5 (one half a d10 roll, rounded up). This is the only move they’re allowed to use. They may otherwise act relatively intelligently, and needn’t use their move at all.

In addition, Trainerless Pokemon cannot benefit from any features that are given to them by their trainer, such as Type Specializations.

<Heading level="3" id="non-trainers-and-pokemon">Non-Trainers and Pokemon</Heading>

Using a Pokeball to catch a Pokemon requires special training and cannot be performed by people without this training. However, a non-Trainer can release a Pokemon from a Pokeball just like a Trainer could. The Pokemon will, however, act like a Trainerless Pokemon.

<Heading level="3" id="limitations-of-commanding-pokemon">Limitations of Commanding Pokemon</Heading>

In order to give a Pokemon an order it can understand, you must be within 60 feet of it. It must be able to hear your order spoken. If it is deafened or the Trainer is unable to speak audibly, it cannot receive the order. For example, a Trainer caught in a Silence spell would be unable to command their Pokemon under most circumstances.

At the DM’s discretion, it may be necessary for the Trainer to be able to see the target of any attacks by the Pokemon.

<Heading level="2" id="pokemon-types-and-modifications-to-types">Pokémon Types and Modifications to Types</Heading>

The Poke5e system uses Pokemon Types for damage, which are naturally rather different than standard D&D damage types. The following chart here that shows what damage type each Pokemon move is most likely to have:

