However, a 1-1 conversion isn’t quite possible, because a Pokemon Move’s type is just as often about what fuels it rather than how it damages an object. For example, Solar Beam is a Grass-Type move, not because of how it harms enemies, but because it’s powered by plant-like photosynthesis. However, these moves are the exceptions rather than the rule, so the DM should just try to catch them when it comes up. It helps that the move damage of Pokemon moves is usually pretty intuitive. For example, Solar Beam is Radiant Damage.

<Heading level="2" id="multiclassing-trainer">Multiclassing Trainer</Heading>

<Heading level="3" id="multiclassing-prerequisites">Prerequisites</Heading>

In order to multiclass into and out of Pokemon Trainer, you must have at least 13 Wisdom and 13 Charisma. This represents your judgement and leadership skills required to be a Trainer.

<Heading level="3" id="multiclassing-into-trainer">Multiclassing into Trainer</Heading>

When you multiclass into Trainer, you do not necessarily immediately gain any of the items associated with Pokemon Trainers, such as Pokeballs or a Pokedex. However, you will know how to use these items and a DM should make these items relatively easy to acquire.

You also do not necessarily gain a starter Pokemon right away, but you do know how to catch Pokemon. It is up to you to hunt down a Pokemon and capture it.

<Heading level="3" id="pokemon-proficiency">Pokémon Proficiency</Heading>

A Pokemon’s Proficiency bonus is equal to their master’s proficiency bonus.

<Heading level="2" id="pokemon-mortality">Pokémon Mortality</Heading>

In Pokemon, explicit death is a rare occurrence, and when it does happen, it’s pretty dramatic. This is in stark contrast to D&D, which often has a fairly blase attitude on death. Adventurers are often constantly in mortal danger and kill very frequently (even good-aligned parties that aren’t “murderhobos”).

Ultimately, this comes down to D&D and Pokemon having different target demographics, and tone. Satoshi, the creator of Pokemon, wanted death to be treated with a lot of weight when it does happen. Gygax, by contrast, is the creator of Tomb of Horrors.

In keeping with the philosophy of Chainmail and Charizards, I consider it necessary that Pokemon are perfectly mortal creatures that can and possibly will die alongside other creatures. This chapter handles mechanics and flavor for Pokemon dying.

<Heading level="3" id="alterations-to-trainer-law">Alterations to Trainer Law</Heading>

When one becomes a Pokemon Trainer, they often do so with a mission to preserve life, and to grow the bond between Trainer and Pokemon for the betterment of both. The purpose of Pokemon Battling is to grow both Trainer and Pokemon, win or lose. To this end, Trainers in Poke5e and the like are strongly discouraged from using Pokemon in any lethal capacity or for having them attack enemies.

However, a Trainer serving alongside adventurers should consider that, under extreme circumstances, these principles are at least temporarily rescinded. The threats a Trainer will face are cruel, dangerous, and difficult to stop. If the enemy threatens sentient life, lethal force must be considered as a last resort. For example, if a Pokemon Trainer finds themselves having to defend a town from an invading orcish army, they are within their right to have their Charmeleon kill the invaders. It is unlikely the attackers will show them or their Pokemon any mercy either.

Likewise, against particularly evil Pokemon Trainers, battles cease to be friendly competitions but life-and-death struggles. Trainers who use their Pokemon for sinister and lethal ends are a vile perversion of the ethos of your standard Pokemon Trainer, and must be stopped.

<Heading level="3" id="pokemon-death">Pokémon Death</Heading>

When a Pokemon is dropped to zero hit points, unless by an enemy deliberately trying to use less-than-lethal force (such as a friendly Pokemon battle), the Pokemon collapses and makes death saving throws like a PC would. If it fails 3 death saving throws, it dies.

Returning a dying Pokemon to its Pokeball does not stabilize it, and it will continue to make death saving throws inside its ball, and may very well die there. Healing spells and moves may target a Pokemon that is in its ball, however.

In addition, while a Pokemon is downed, you may send out another. If the downed pokemon is stabilized, you may return it or a new one to their Pokeball.

<Heading level="3" id="trainer-morale">Trainer Morale</Heading>

The bond between Trainer and Pokemon is very strong. A Pokemon’s death will almost invariably be a traumatizing experience for a Trainer, and it will weigh heavily on their mind long after. It is common for Trainers who lose a Pokemon to consider it in some manner their fault, and they may be haunted by the question of if there were any choices they could have made that would have saved them.

If you are using any kind of morale or sanity rules, of which many have been made, a Trainer losing a Pokemon should be a serious blow to their sanity, almost as bad as a humanoid friend dying.

<Heading level="3" id="trainer-legacy">Trainer's Legacy</Heading>

So, let’s examine the reverse scenario. The Trainer has died, but some of their Pokemon survive.

Other Trainers can pick up the Pokemon off their corpses and use them. Alternatively, at the DM’s discretion, there may be some kind of lock on the Pokeballs that requires them to be ‘unlocked’ first. A machine similar to a Trading Machine can pull this off.

At the DM’s discretion, there may be a loyalty penalty for Trainers who were strangers (or even enemies) to the deceased Trainer. However, if they were friends, especially if they knew each other well, there should be no penalty at all.

If there are no other Trainers in the party, they may still attempt to use the Pokemon, but the Pokemon are still considered “Trainerless”.
